One possible configuration interface could be...

  doormove=bump:{y#nq},untrap:{y#nq},open:{y#nq},unlock:{y#nq}

You would have the option to define for every action whether
- you shall always be asked for an explicit action ('q', query),
- you never want that action to be implicitly done ('n', no), or
- the game shall try the action for a number of turns ('y#', yes).

A concrete configuration may be...

  doormove=bump:n,untrap:y8,open:y5,unlock:n

with the meaning; don't bump into door (ie. do not use up a turn),
but immediately try (up to) 8 times to find traps (interruptable if
monsters approach), then try to open the door for at most 5 turns,
but never try to unlock it (that requires explicit unlocking then).

You could also add another action 'kick' (some people might like
that :-).

Even the order of actions could be defined to be meaningful...

  doormove=open:y5,kick:y
  # try five times/turns to open the door, then kick it down

  doormove=kick:y1,unlock:y3,open:y
  # if it can't be kicked the first time then try unlocking and
  # opening

  These examples are quite exotic (a standard configuration
  will usually be simpler), but show the flexibility based on
  the already existing building blocks.

Well, that's just off top of my head, but it provides flexibility
while still using existing code and the game offers known behaviour
with respect to the primitives (kick, untrap, open, unlock).


